1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,530
Only about one dollar in ten

2
00:00:01,530 --> 00:00:03,230
in America is actual cash.

3
00:00:03,730 --> 00:00:04,630
The other nine?

4
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:06,910
They were typed into existence by

5
00:00:06,710 --> 00:00:07,190
banks,

6
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:08,150
when they made loans.

7
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:11,390
That's not a conspiracy theory.

8
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:12,950
The Bank of England said it

9
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:13,450
in 2014.

10
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:15,170
And once you see how it

11
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:15,430
works,

12
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:16,690
you'll never look at your bank

13
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,730
balance the same way.

14
00:00:18,316 --> 00:00:19,436
In the next ten minutes:

15
00:00:19,596 --> 00:00:20,796
how a loan creates money,

16
00:00:21,176 --> 00:00:22,756
how paying it back destroys it,

17
00:00:22,996 --> 00:00:24,796
what stops banks from creating infinite

18
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:25,216
money,

19
00:00:25,336 --> 00:00:26,376
and what it all means for

20
00:00:26,176 --> 00:00:26,616
you.

21
00:00:27,917 --> 00:00:29,397
Most of us learned a simple

22
00:00:29,197 --> 00:00:29,817
story.

23
00:00:30,277 --> 00:00:31,497
You put a hundred dollars in

24
00:00:31,297 --> 00:00:31,797
the bank.

25
00:00:31,977 --> 00:00:33,277
The bank keeps a little and

26
00:00:33,077 --> 00:00:34,497
lends the rest to someone else.

27
00:00:35,129 --> 00:00:36,629
In the textbook version,

28
00:00:36,709 --> 00:00:37,689
called the money multiplier,

29
00:00:38,129 --> 00:00:39,569
the bank keeps ten percent and

30
00:00:39,369 --> 00:00:40,109
lends out ninety.

31
00:00:40,669 --> 00:00:42,189
That ninety lands in another bank,

32
00:00:42,309 --> 00:00:43,189
which lends out eighty-one,

33
00:00:43,409 --> 00:00:44,089
and so on.

34
00:00:44,722 --> 00:00:45,462
It sounds neat.

35
00:00:45,702 --> 00:00:46,842
But in 2014,

36
00:00:47,142 --> 00:00:48,602
the Bank of England published a

37
00:00:48,402 --> 00:00:50,502
paper called Money creation in the

38
00:00:50,302 --> 00:00:51,062
modern economy,

39
00:00:51,522 --> 00:00:52,822
and it says that isn't how

40
00:00:52,622 --> 00:00:53,762
banks work in practice.

41
00:00:54,543 --> 00:00:55,703
Banks don't wait for your deposit

42
00:00:55,503 --> 00:00:56,403
to make a loan.

43
00:00:56,603 --> 00:00:58,143
The loan makes the deposit.

44
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:00,810
Let's watch it happen.

45
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:01,710
Meet Maya.

46
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:03,190
She wants to buy a house,

47
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:04,450
and asks her bank for a

48
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:05,930
300,000 dollar mortgage.

49
00:01:06,689 --> 00:01:08,309
The bank checks her income and

50
00:01:08,109 --> 00:01:08,749
her credit.

51
00:01:09,029 --> 00:01:09,829
Then it says yes.

52
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:12,450
Now here's the trick.

53
00:01:12,890 --> 00:01:14,070
The bank doesn't open a vault

54
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:15,510
and hand her someone else's savings.

55
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:18,010
It types 300,000 dollars into Maya's

56
00:01:17,810 --> 00:01:18,230
account.

57
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:19,760
On the bank's books,

58
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:20,720
two lines appear.

59
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:21,960
Something it owns:

60
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:24,140
Maya owes the bank 300,000 dollars.

61
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:25,800
And something it owes:

62
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,800
the bank owes Maya 300,000 dollars.

63
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:29,460
That's her new deposit.

64
00:01:30,306 --> 00:01:31,926
The two lines balance.

65
00:01:31,726 --> 00:01:34,206
And 300,000 dollars of brand-new money

66
00:01:34,006 --> 00:01:35,666
now exists that didn't exist a

67
00:01:35,466 --> 00:01:36,166
minute ago.

68
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:38,817
Maya pays the seller.

69
00:01:39,077 --> 00:01:40,397
The money moves from her bank

70
00:01:40,197 --> 00:01:41,277
to the seller's bank,

71
00:01:41,377 --> 00:01:42,337
but it doesn't disappear.

72
00:01:43,065 --> 00:01:44,285
It's now part of the money

73
00:01:44,085 --> 00:01:44,565
supply:

74
00:01:44,845 --> 00:01:46,005
money that you and I could

75
00:01:45,805 --> 00:01:46,585
end up holding.

76
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:50,103
And here's the part almost nobody

77
00:01:49,903 --> 00:01:50,443
knows.

78
00:01:50,723 --> 00:01:52,123
When Maya repays her loan,

79
00:01:52,303 --> 00:01:53,303
that money is destroyed.

80
00:01:54,053 --> 00:01:55,993
Each payment cancels part of both

81
00:01:55,793 --> 00:01:56,273
lines.

82
00:01:56,773 --> 00:01:57,633
Her debt shrinks,

83
00:01:57,433 --> 00:01:58,973
and the deposit she paid with

84
00:01:58,773 --> 00:01:59,393
is gone.

85
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:01,341
The interest is different.

86
00:02:01,721 --> 00:02:02,821
That's the bank's income.

87
00:02:03,241 --> 00:02:04,081
It pays salaries,

88
00:02:04,281 --> 00:02:05,141
it pays savers,

89
00:02:05,201 --> 00:02:06,301
and the rest is profit.

90
00:02:07,717 --> 00:02:09,057
And that interest adds up.

91
00:02:09,177 --> 00:02:10,097
In late October,

92
00:02:10,277 --> 00:02:12,197
the average 30-year mortgage rate was

93
00:02:11,997 --> 00:02:13,477
about 7.3 percent.

94
00:02:14,188 --> 00:02:15,048
At that rate,

95
00:02:15,208 --> 00:02:17,608
Maya's 300,000 dollar loan costs about

96
00:02:17,408 --> 00:02:19,468
2,061 dollars a month.

97
00:02:20,068 --> 00:02:20,868
Over thirty years,

98
00:02:20,748 --> 00:02:23,055
she pays back about 742 thousand

99
00:02:22,855 --> 00:02:23,308
dollars,

100
00:02:23,108 --> 00:02:25,928
and about 442 thousand of that

101
00:02:25,728 --> 00:02:26,328
is interest.

102
00:02:27,099 --> 00:02:27,719
Meanwhile,

103
00:02:27,939 --> 00:02:29,599
the average savings account pays about

104
00:02:29,399 --> 00:02:31,299
0.38 percent a year.

105
00:02:31,719 --> 00:02:32,279
That gap,

106
00:02:32,079 --> 00:02:34,319
between what banks charge borrowers and

107
00:02:34,119 --> 00:02:35,119
what they pay savers,

108
00:02:34,919 --> 00:02:36,339
is a big part of how

109
00:02:36,139 --> 00:02:37,059
banks make money.

110
00:02:38,383 --> 00:02:40,423
So money is always being born

111
00:02:40,223 --> 00:02:40,963
and destroyed.

112
00:02:41,443 --> 00:02:42,563
New loans create it.

113
00:02:42,923 --> 00:02:44,083
Repayments erase it.

114
00:02:45,417 --> 00:02:47,197
So where does physical cash come

115
00:02:46,997 --> 00:02:47,397
in?

116
00:02:47,957 --> 00:02:48,557
In the US,

117
00:02:48,617 --> 00:02:49,937
paper money is printed by the

118
00:02:49,737 --> 00:02:50,177
Treasury,

119
00:02:49,977 --> 00:02:52,037
and the Federal Reserve hands it

120
00:02:51,837 --> 00:02:52,497
out to banks.

121
00:02:53,241 --> 00:02:54,941
But cash only reaches you when

122
00:02:54,741 --> 00:02:55,701
you take it out of your

123
00:02:55,501 --> 00:02:55,941
account.

124
00:02:56,561 --> 00:02:58,021
Withdraw a hundred dollars from an

125
00:02:57,821 --> 00:02:58,241
ATM,

126
00:02:58,041 --> 00:03:00,201
and your bank balance drops by

127
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:00,481
a hundred.

128
00:03:01,233 --> 00:03:03,073
No new money was created.

129
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:04,573
It just changed form:

130
00:03:04,373 --> 00:03:05,933
from a number on a screen

131
00:03:05,733 --> 00:03:07,273
to paper in your hand.

132
00:03:08,817 --> 00:03:10,377
So how much money is out

133
00:03:10,177 --> 00:03:10,577
there?

134
00:03:10,957 --> 00:03:11,637
In the US,

135
00:03:11,777 --> 00:03:14,077
the broad measure is called M2:

136
00:03:13,877 --> 00:03:14,197
cash,

137
00:03:14,397 --> 00:03:16,017
plus checking and savings accounts,

138
00:03:16,137 --> 00:03:17,517
plus a few similar accounts.

139
00:03:18,291 --> 00:03:20,031
In September 2026,

140
00:03:20,231 --> 00:03:23,091
M2 was about 23.4 trillion dollars.

141
00:03:24,199 --> 00:03:25,659
Physical cash in circulation?

142
00:03:25,979 --> 00:03:27,319
About 2.5 trillion.

143
00:03:27,499 --> 00:03:28,719
That's the one dollar in ten.

144
00:03:29,236 --> 00:03:30,476
In 1960,

145
00:03:30,636 --> 00:03:33,356
M2 was about 304 billion dollars.

146
00:03:34,176 --> 00:03:34,716
Today,

147
00:03:34,876 --> 00:03:36,616
it's more than seventy times bigger.

148
00:03:37,315 --> 00:03:39,035
The economy and prices grew a

149
00:03:38,835 --> 00:03:40,355
lot over those decades too.

150
00:03:40,715 --> 00:03:42,015
But most of that money was

151
00:03:41,815 --> 00:03:43,835
created the same way Maya's was:

152
00:03:43,635 --> 00:03:45,075
by banks making loans.

153
00:03:46,583 --> 00:03:47,263
Right now,

154
00:03:47,063 --> 00:03:49,963
US commercial banks hold about 19.8

155
00:03:49,763 --> 00:03:51,263
trillion dollars of deposits,

156
00:03:51,063 --> 00:03:54,183
and about 14.2 trillion dollars of

157
00:03:53,983 --> 00:03:54,443
loans.

158
00:03:55,014 --> 00:03:56,354
Most of the rest of what

159
00:03:56,154 --> 00:03:57,814
they own is government bonds,

160
00:03:58,054 --> 00:03:58,794
other securities,

161
00:03:59,014 --> 00:04:00,254
and reserves at the Fed.

162
00:04:01,617 --> 00:04:03,317
So if banks can type money

163
00:04:03,117 --> 00:04:03,897
into existence,

164
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:06,277
why don't they create infinite amounts?

165
00:04:07,091 --> 00:04:08,591
You might think it's the reserve

166
00:04:08,391 --> 00:04:09,091
requirement:

167
00:04:09,351 --> 00:04:10,951
the rule that banks must keep

168
00:04:10,751 --> 00:04:12,271
a slice of their deposits at

169
00:04:12,071 --> 00:04:12,851
the central bank.

170
00:04:13,381 --> 00:04:14,281
In the US,

171
00:04:14,081 --> 00:04:16,081
that requirement has been zero since

172
00:04:15,881 --> 00:04:16,841
March 2020.

173
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,490
Banks still hold about 3 trillion

174
00:04:19,290 --> 00:04:20,940
dollars of reserves anyway,

175
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:23,060
because they need cash on hand

176
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:24,460
to pay each other and their

177
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:24,760
customers.

178
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:27,400
So the requirement isn't what holds

179
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:27,900
them back.

180
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:30,490
The real limits are different.

181
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:31,330
Limit one:

182
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:31,950
borrowers.

183
00:04:32,630 --> 00:04:33,930
A bank needs people it trusts

184
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:34,830
to pay it back.

185
00:04:35,050 --> 00:04:36,250
When a borrower can't pay,

186
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:37,470
the bank takes the loss.

187
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:38,932
Limit two:

188
00:04:38,992 --> 00:04:39,412
capital.

189
00:04:39,952 --> 00:04:41,652
Regulators make banks fund part of

190
00:04:41,452 --> 00:04:42,932
their loans with their own money,

191
00:04:43,032 --> 00:04:44,052
the shareholders' money,

192
00:04:44,012 --> 00:04:45,392
as a cushion against losses.

193
00:04:46,213 --> 00:04:47,093
Limit three:

194
00:04:47,213 --> 00:04:47,893
interest rates.

195
00:04:48,393 --> 00:04:49,933
The Federal Reserve sets the rate

196
00:04:49,733 --> 00:04:51,213
that ripples through every loan.

197
00:04:51,593 --> 00:04:52,513
When rates go up,

198
00:04:52,793 --> 00:04:53,573
borrowing gets expensive,

199
00:04:53,973 --> 00:04:54,893
fewer people borrow,

200
00:04:55,013 --> 00:04:56,473
and less new money gets created.

201
00:04:57,256 --> 00:04:58,476
And limit four:

202
00:04:58,276 --> 00:04:59,116
confidence.

203
00:04:59,776 --> 00:05:01,156
Your deposit is a promise.

204
00:05:01,756 --> 00:05:03,196
If too many people want that

205
00:05:02,996 --> 00:05:04,136
promise paid at once,

206
00:05:04,496 --> 00:05:05,836
a bank can run out of

207
00:05:05,636 --> 00:05:06,076
cash.

208
00:05:07,517 --> 00:05:09,177
That's exactly what happened to Silicon

209
00:05:08,977 --> 00:05:11,277
Valley Bank in March 2023.

210
00:05:11,859 --> 00:05:12,979
The bank had put a lot

211
00:05:12,779 --> 00:05:14,539
of its customers' money into long-term

212
00:05:14,339 --> 00:05:14,759
bonds.

213
00:05:15,179 --> 00:05:16,199
When interest rates jumped,

214
00:05:16,319 --> 00:05:17,459
those bonds lost value,

215
00:05:17,539 --> 00:05:18,519
and when customers heard,

216
00:05:18,639 --> 00:05:19,639
they rushed to get their money

217
00:05:19,439 --> 00:05:19,919
out.

218
00:05:20,468 --> 00:05:21,508
On March 9,

219
00:05:21,528 --> 00:05:23,068
customers tried to pull out 42

220
00:05:22,868 --> 00:05:24,928
billion dollars in a single day,

221
00:05:25,128 --> 00:05:26,768
according to the Federal Reserve's own

222
00:05:26,568 --> 00:05:27,028
review.

223
00:05:27,648 --> 00:05:28,368
The next day,

224
00:05:28,368 --> 00:05:29,468
the bank was shut down.

225
00:05:30,683 --> 00:05:32,103
That's why the US has deposit

226
00:05:31,903 --> 00:05:32,663
insurance.

227
00:05:33,263 --> 00:05:35,839
The FDIC covers up to 250,000

228
00:05:35,639 --> 00:05:37,203
dollars per depositor,

229
00:05:37,003 --> 00:05:37,703
per bank,

230
00:05:37,503 --> 00:05:39,443
for each type of account ownership.

231
00:05:40,104 --> 00:05:42,184
Most people's money sits well under

232
00:05:41,984 --> 00:05:42,604
that limit.

233
00:05:42,884 --> 00:05:44,284
But it's worth knowing the rule.

234
00:05:45,583 --> 00:05:47,283
So where does the Federal Reserve

235
00:05:47,083 --> 00:05:47,843
come in?

236
00:05:48,283 --> 00:05:49,903
The Fed creates a different kind

237
00:05:49,703 --> 00:05:50,323
of money:

238
00:05:50,603 --> 00:05:50,803
reserves,

239
00:05:51,443 --> 00:05:52,763
the money banks use to pay

240
00:05:52,563 --> 00:05:53,143
each other.

241
00:05:53,623 --> 00:05:55,303
It mostly does this by buying

242
00:05:55,103 --> 00:05:56,843
government bonds and mortgage bonds,

243
00:05:56,643 --> 00:05:58,583
and paying for them with brand-new

244
00:05:58,383 --> 00:05:58,883
reserves.

245
00:05:59,543 --> 00:06:01,403
That's called quantitative easing.

246
00:06:01,929 --> 00:06:02,989
In March 2020,

247
00:06:02,849 --> 00:06:04,289
as the pandemic hit,

248
00:06:04,429 --> 00:06:05,929
the Fed's balance sheet was about

249
00:06:05,729 --> 00:06:07,589
4.2 trillion dollars.

250
00:06:07,969 --> 00:06:08,969
By April 2022,

251
00:06:09,109 --> 00:06:10,449
it had more than doubled,

252
00:06:10,249 --> 00:06:11,729
to almost 9 trillion.

253
00:06:12,381 --> 00:06:13,521
At the same time,

254
00:06:13,601 --> 00:06:15,001
the government sent out stimulus,

255
00:06:15,281 --> 00:06:16,261
banks kept lending,

256
00:06:16,461 --> 00:06:17,681
and deposits poured in.

257
00:06:18,917 --> 00:06:21,257
From February 2020 to March 2022,

258
00:06:21,277 --> 00:06:23,657
M2 jumped about 41 percent,

259
00:06:23,457 --> 00:06:26,597
from 15.5 to 21.8 trillion dollars.

260
00:06:27,267 --> 00:06:28,607
In February 2021,

261
00:06:28,947 --> 00:06:30,947
it was growing almost 27 percent

262
00:06:30,747 --> 00:06:31,307
a year:

263
00:06:31,607 --> 00:06:33,127
the fastest pace in data going

264
00:06:32,927 --> 00:06:34,127
back to 1960.

265
00:06:34,877 --> 00:06:36,137
And what came next?

266
00:06:36,477 --> 00:06:38,377
Inflation hit about 9 percent in

267
00:06:38,177 --> 00:06:39,297
June 2022,

268
00:06:39,557 --> 00:06:41,257
the highest in about four decades.

269
00:06:41,948 --> 00:06:43,848
Economists still argue about how much

270
00:06:43,648 --> 00:06:44,668
of that was the money,

271
00:06:44,468 --> 00:06:46,288
and how much was supply chains

272
00:06:46,088 --> 00:06:46,708
and energy.

273
00:06:47,208 --> 00:06:48,448
But the timing is hard to

274
00:06:48,248 --> 00:06:48,788
ignore.

275
00:06:50,217 --> 00:06:51,037
Since then,

276
00:06:51,277 --> 00:06:52,457
the Fed has shrunk its balance

277
00:06:52,257 --> 00:06:54,397
sheet to about 6.7 trillion dollars,

278
00:06:54,977 --> 00:06:57,387
and M2 is about 23.4 trillion.

279
00:06:58,168 --> 00:06:59,948
And something rare happened as interest

280
00:06:59,748 --> 00:07:00,528
rates rose.

281
00:07:00,928 --> 00:07:03,468
From December 2022 to early 2024,

282
00:07:03,708 --> 00:07:05,108
M2 was smaller than a year

283
00:07:04,908 --> 00:07:05,368
before:

284
00:07:05,888 --> 00:07:07,228
the first yearly drops in data

285
00:07:07,028 --> 00:07:08,408
going back to 1960.

286
00:07:09,008 --> 00:07:09,748
Fewer new loans,

287
00:07:09,948 --> 00:07:10,708
more repayments,

288
00:07:10,508 --> 00:07:12,388
so money was destroyed faster than

289
00:07:12,188 --> 00:07:12,848
it was created.

290
00:07:13,545 --> 00:07:15,725
And inflation today is about 4

291
00:07:15,525 --> 00:07:16,025
percent.

292
00:07:17,417 --> 00:07:18,997
So what does all this mean

293
00:07:18,797 --> 00:07:19,377
for you?

294
00:07:19,937 --> 00:07:20,317
Three things.

295
00:07:20,877 --> 00:07:22,177
This isn't financial advice,

296
00:07:22,297 --> 00:07:23,517
just how the system works.

297
00:07:24,413 --> 00:07:24,873
One:

298
00:07:25,053 --> 00:07:26,333
the money in your bank account

299
00:07:26,133 --> 00:07:27,433
is a promise from your bank,

300
00:07:27,233 --> 00:07:29,873
insured up to 250,000 dollars.

301
00:07:30,273 --> 00:07:31,393
That's why the limit matters.

302
00:07:32,326 --> 00:07:32,826
Two:

303
00:07:32,966 --> 00:07:33,686
when you borrow,

304
00:07:33,966 --> 00:07:35,806
you're not borrowing someone else's savings.

305
00:07:36,306 --> 00:07:37,426
You're creating new money,

306
00:07:37,506 --> 00:07:38,886
and the interest you pay is

307
00:07:38,686 --> 00:07:40,026
how the bank earns from it.

308
00:07:40,534 --> 00:07:40,994
Three:

309
00:07:41,274 --> 00:07:43,034
when new money is created faster

310
00:07:42,834 --> 00:07:44,334
than the economy grows,

311
00:07:44,654 --> 00:07:45,674
prices can rise,

312
00:07:45,474 --> 00:07:47,894
and cash sitting still slowly loses

313
00:07:47,694 --> 00:07:48,314
value.

314
00:07:48,954 --> 00:07:50,974
Our inflation video shows exactly how

315
00:07:50,774 --> 00:07:51,194
much.

316
00:07:52,583 --> 00:07:53,403
Let's recap.

317
00:07:53,763 --> 00:07:55,563
Only about one dollar in ten

318
00:07:55,363 --> 00:07:56,063
is cash.

319
00:07:56,883 --> 00:07:58,703
Banks create most money by making

320
00:07:58,503 --> 00:07:59,043
loans,

321
00:07:58,843 --> 00:08:00,703
and repayments destroy it.

322
00:08:01,143 --> 00:08:02,823
The reserve requirement is zero,

323
00:08:02,963 --> 00:08:04,603
so what really limits banks is

324
00:08:04,403 --> 00:08:04,943
borrowers,

325
00:08:05,363 --> 00:08:05,743
capital,

326
00:08:06,063 --> 00:08:06,743
interest rates,

327
00:08:06,543 --> 00:08:07,303
and trust.

328
00:08:07,958 --> 00:08:08,938
So here's my question:

329
00:08:09,098 --> 00:08:10,138
did you know banks create money

330
00:08:09,938 --> 00:08:10,618
when they lend?

331
00:08:10,998 --> 00:08:11,598
Yes or no,

332
00:08:11,658 --> 00:08:12,478
tell me in the comments.

333
00:08:12,278 --> 00:08:13,238
And subscribe,

334
00:08:13,038 --> 00:08:14,638
because on Monday we look at

335
00:08:14,438 --> 00:08:14,838
gold:

336
00:08:14,638 --> 00:08:16,498
down 23 percent from its record.

337
00:08:16,618 --> 00:08:17,078
What happened?

338
00:08:17,683 --> 00:08:19,783
This video is for education only.

339
00:08:20,243 --> 00:08:21,543
It's not financial advice.

